.TH NTPDATE 1
.SH NAME
ntpdate \- query network time or set the guest clock
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ntpdate
.RI [ -d ] " [-s] [-p PORT] [-o HOURS] SERVER"
.SH DESCRIPTION
Resolve SERVER and send an NTP client request, using UDP port 123 by default.
Display the returned calendar time. -s also sets the guest's UTC calendar
and requires root. -d is accepted for the display mode. -o shifts only the
display by the requested whole hours, from -12 through 14; clock updates
always use UTC. The host's clock is never modified.
.PP
Replies must match the request timestamp and peer, use NTP version 3 or 4
server mode, and report a synchronized stratum from 1 through 15. Queries
are bounded to three attempts with two-second monotonic timeouts. Clock
updates do not alter networking deadlines or uptime. NTP authentication,
clock discipline and subsecond correction are not supplied.
.SH SEE ALSO
date(1), uptime(1), host(1)
